---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';
const app = appBySlug('firepush')!;
const path = '/apps/firepush';
const title = 'Firepush for Shopify: coordinate web push and SMS';
const desc = 'Evaluate Firepush as an SMS and browser notification tool by testing independent permissions, cart exits and alert expiry.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← App procurement directory</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Operating review · checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <article class="container prose-shopify pb-16">

    <h2>Two delivery paths, two permission states</h2>
    <p>The <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/firepush">current Firepush Shopify listing</a> advertises SMS and web push, automated cart recovery, price alerts and delivery updates. That scope makes it a candidate for event-driven notifications; it does not establish a full email lifecycle offering.</p>
    <p>The operating question is whether your store can use both paths without confusing permission or doubling reminders. Browser permission applies to a browser context. An SMS subscription belongs to a phone-number permission record. A shopper may qualify for one, both or neither. Do not convert a browser opt-in into permission to text.</p>
    <h2>A proposed permission matrix</h2>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Test state</th><th>Expected routing rule to configure</th><th>Inspect</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Push allowed, SMS absent</td><td>Only eligible browser notifications</td><td>No phone audience inferred from browser permission</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SMS allowed, browser denied</td><td>Only eligible text messages</td><td>No repeated browser prompts used as a fallback</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Both allowed</td><td>A deliberate channel sequence with a stop condition</td><td>No duplicate offer from overlapping triggers</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SMS opted out, push unchanged</td><td>Honor the recorded channel preferences and any broader customer request</td><td>Suppression applied before the next queued send</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Browser permission revoked</td><td>Stop delivery attempts through that permission state</td><td>Unreachable browser is not silently treated as SMS consent</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Run the matrix on the devices and browsers your shoppers actually use. Save screenshots of permission states and queue decisions. A delivered notification on one desktop is not evidence that mobile users or another browser will receive the same experience.</p>
    <h2>Cart recovery: stop after purchase</h2>
    <p>Create a test cart and queue a reminder. Complete the order before the scheduled delivery and inspect whether the reminder is cancelled. Repeat after changing the cart contents, and inspect the destination link so the customer does not land on a stale checkout. If another email or SMS platform also owns cart recovery, pause one path during this rehearsal.</p>
    <p>Choose a primary reminder path for shoppers who permit both channels. If you want a fallback, define what event permits it and when it expires. “Push was not clicked” is not the same as “push was never delivered”; inspect which signals the app exposes before basing escalation on them.</p>
    <h2>Price and stock alerts need an expiry rule</h2>
    <p>For a proposed price-alert test, change a variant price, queue the alert, then restore the old price before delivery. Record whether the payload and destination still match the live offer. If the app cannot recheck at delivery, choose an operating process that cancels stale campaigns rather than promising a price you can no longer honor.</p>
    <p>Repeat with an unavailable variant and with a customer who purchased after subscribing. Decide whether the alert is still useful for that customer. Avoid invented scarcity: stock or deadline claims should come from a current catalog condition that an operator can verify.</p>
    <h2>Separate service updates from promotions</h2>
    <p>A delivery notification and a sale announcement have different purposes. Map each template to its trigger, permission basis and owner. Confirm which delivery events your installation actually receives before replacing a working order-update process. Have support review the reply path so shoppers with a delivery problem reach a person or a monitored queue.</p>
    <h2>Cost and measurement by channel</h2>
    <p>Get the current push allowance and SMS rates for the destination markets you serve. Do not interpret included web pushes as included text messages. Model the expected notifications from each trigger, failed or repeated sends, SMS message segmentation and support workload. Keep email costs separate if another platform still handles email.</p>
    <p>Track eligible audience, attempted delivery, reachable browsers, SMS opt-outs and purchases as separate measures. A larger browser subscriber count is not automatically a larger reachable phone audience. To evaluate incremental value, propose a holdout that avoids overlapping reminders from other senders and compare contribution after message costs, discounts and returns. No measured lift is asserted here.</p>
    <h2>When Firepush belongs on the shortlist</h2>
    <p>Consider it when SMS plus browser alerts solve a defined event-notification job and you can assign someone to maintain those rules. If the requirement is email education, complex cross-platform profiles or an existing service desk workflow, specify those dependencies separately. Keep the tool only if the demonstrated installation can satisfy the routing matrix and cancellation tests you need.</p>

    <h2>Catalog acceptance brief</h2>
    <p>{app.shopifyFit}</p>
    <p>This is a proposed evaluation, not an observed merchant test or a claim that the platform passed it. Save the evidence, unresolved exceptions and responsible owner before approving a change.</p>
    <h2>Continue the decision</h2>
    <p>Return to the <a class="link" href="/apps">procurement directory</a> to compare operating requirements. Use the <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing worksheet</a> for your actual quote and the <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination guide</a> when more than one sender remains active.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
